(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 4, part: `Part Two: The Floor`, title: `The Floor`, weeks: `Months 3 to 4`,
  tasks: [`C1`,`C3`,`C4`,`C2`,`E6`,`E5`,`E2`,`SA4`,`L6`],
  cast: [
    [`Denise Harmon`,`Nurse manager at Cheyenne. Fought the Penwright launch, then became its best champion. Now a One Chart superuser.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Highway 287 between Fort Collins and Laramie has two moods in January. On Monday morning it was in the bad one: wind out of the west, snow moving sideways across the road in long white ropes, a pickup in the ditch near the state line with its flashers on and nobody in it.`,
    `Maria drove it at 5:40 AM with the radio off. On the passenger seat were two sets of scrubs, her badge, a stethoscope she hadn't used in four years and had brought anyway, and a printed copy of Walt Hagen's complaint. She'd read it enough times to know it by heart. Forty-seven clicks, up from forty-one.`,
    `She knew where the six new clicks had come from. A screen that made the discharging doctor confirm he'd seen every result still pending. A count of medications on every transfer, so a list that came up short would get noticed. Both came out of the Laramie review in December. Both were good ideas. Both were hers.`,
    `The plan for the week fit on an index card. Laramie Monday through Wednesday. Rawlins Thursday. Cheyenne Friday. No slides, no town halls. Scrubs, a legal pad, and the night shift's coffee.`,
    `Laramie's med-surg charge nurse met her at the elevator at 6:50. Renee had twenty years on the unit and the posture of someone who'd been told a vice president was coming and had decided to reserve judgment. She handed Maria a handheld scanner, still warm from the charger.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="woman">"You remember how?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Wristband first,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Then the med. Then you look at the patient anyway."</span>`,
    `Renee nodded once, which seemed to settle something. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Then you can follow Jess at nine. And Dr. Hagen's waiting for you in the surgeons' lounge. He's been there since seven. He brought a pencil."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: the bedside in words and numbers`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The words on the order`,
        body: [`The day before, a Sunday, Ochoa had bought her breakfast at the Greeley diner and taken her legal pad without asking. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"You already speak bedside,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Your analysts mostly don't, and your board doesn't at all. This week, listen like an analyst. Write down every word somebody has to translate."</span>`,
          `A medication order has a drug, a dose, a route, a frequency, and sometimes a reason. Frequencies come in two kinds. Some count doses per day: daily, BID for twice a day, TID for three times, QID for four. Those are given at the hospital's standard times, set by policy. Others count hours: q4h, q6h, q8h, q12h mean every four, six, eight, or twelve hours, around the clock. PRN means as needed, and a good PRN order says for what and how often at most. STAT means now. AC is before meals, PC after meals, HS at bedtime.`,
          `Ochoa wrote BID equals q12h. Maria took the pen and crossed it out. BID is twice a day at set times, maybe nine and nine. q12h is every twelve hours from the first dose, and some drugs need exactly that. Ochoa looked at the line for a second, then wrote it her way.`,
          `Routes say how the drug gets in. PO is by mouth, SL under the tongue, IV into a vein, IM into a muscle, SubQ under the skin, PR rectally, plus inhaled, topical, transdermal, and by feeding tube. Clinical content is also sorted by body system: cardiovascular, respiratory, renal, gastrointestinal, neurologic, endocrine, musculoskeletal, hematologic, and integumentary, which is the skin. Order catalogs, problem lists, and reports are built on these words, so a word mapped wrong moves real doses.`,
          `Some shorthand causes errors often enough that The Joint Commission keeps an official do-not-use list for handwritten, free-text, and preprinted orders and medication documentation. U gets misread as a zero or a four, so write units. IU gets mistaken for IV or 10, so write international units. QD and QOD get confused, so write daily and every other day. A trailing zero turns 1.0 mg into 10, though lab values and the sizes of lesions or tubes are exceptions. A missing leading zero turns .5 into 5. MS can mean morphine sulfate or magnesium sulfate, and MSO4 and MgSO4 are on the list too, so spell them out. Pick lists preprogrammed into an EHR aren't formally covered, but hospitals are expected to strip these terms whenever they build or upgrade one. A longer list from ISMP, the Institute for Safe Medication Practices, adds shorthand like HS and SQ, and many hospitals follow it too.`],
        terms: [[`PRN`,`As needed. A complete PRN order states the reason and the minimum time between doses.`],[`BID versus q12h`,`BID is twice a day at standard administration times. q12h is every twelve hours by the clock.`],[`Do-not-use list`,`The Joint Commission's official list of abbreviations that must not be used in handwritten, free-text, or preprinted orders and medication documentation. It doesn't formally cover preprogrammed EHR pick lists, but organizations are expected to remove the terms when they build or upgrade.`]],
        exam: `Expect to decode an order or spot an unsafe abbreviation. Know the frequencies, the routes, and the do-not-use items, and know that standard administration times are a policy decision.` },
      { h: `The loop, and where it breaks`,
        body: [`Ochoa drew a circle. Closed loop medication management means every step checks the one before it. The prescriber enters the order in CPOE, where decision support checks allergies, interactions, and dose ranges while the prescriber can still change it. A pharmacist verifies it. A profiled automated dispensing cabinet releases only what's been verified for that patient. At the bedside the nurse scans the wristband and the medication, and BCMA checks them against the eMAR, the electronic record of what's due and what was given. A smart pump can take the rate from the order instead of from a nurse's fingers.`,
          `The checks are the five rights: right patient, right drug, right dose, right route, right time, with right documentation and right reason often added. Every break in the loop is a place a check gets skipped. Paper orders a clerk types in skip the prescriber's alerts. A cabinet on override skips the pharmacist. A dose charted without a scan skips the bedside check. The loop has to survive a transfer, too. At every transition of care, medication reconciliation compares a complete list of what the patient was taking with what's ordered now. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"A workaround isn't a bad nurse,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"It's the system telling you where it fights the work. Find out why before you take it away."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Closed loop medication management`,`Linked CPOE, pharmacist verification, dispensing, BCMA, and eMAR, so each step checks the previous one.`],[`eMAR`,`Electronic medication administration record. The record of what's scheduled and what was given.`],[`Workaround`,`A way around a designed process. A signal of a design or equipment problem to analyze.`]],
        exam: `When a question describes staff working around BCMA or CPOE, the best answer finds and fixes the cause. Punishing staff or removing the override usually makes things worse.` },
      { h: `Counting the floor`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Every metric is a fraction,"</span> Ochoa said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Read the bottom before you believe the top."</span> Average daily census is total inpatient days in a period divided by the days in the period. Occupancy is census divided by staffed beds. Average length of stay is formally the total days of the stays that ended in the period divided by those discharges. Inpatient days divided by discharges is a common shortcut. Each one depends on who counts as an inpatient. Observation patients and swing bed patients are counted differently in different reports.`,
          `Turnaround time needs a defined start and stop: order to result, order to administration, door to antibiotic. Report the median, and a slow percentile like the 90th, because an average hides the patients who waited longest. BCMA scan compliance is doses where both the wristband and the medication were scanned, divided by all doses given. Adherence is the share of cases where the expected step happened, like an order set used or a protocol followed.`],
        terms: [[`Average daily census`,`Total inpatient days in a period divided by the number of days in the period.`],[`BCMA scan compliance`,`Doses with both patient and medication scanned, divided by all doses administered.`],[`Turnaround time`,`The time between two defined events, such as order and administration.`]],
        exam: `Expect a calculation and a trap. If a rate looks too good, check what the denominator leaves out.` },
      { h: `Draw it before you fix it`,
        body: [`A process map shows the steps. In a flowchart, ovals start and end it, rectangles are steps, and diamonds are decisions. A swimlane map puts each role in its own lane, so the handoffs show. Map the current state as people actually do it, with the people who do it, then the future state you want. The difference between them is your gap analysis.`],
        terms: [[`Swimlane map`,`A process map with a lane for each role or department, showing who does each step and where work changes hands.`],[`Current state`,`The process as it runs today, mapped from observation.`]],
        exam: `When the question asks how to see who does each step and where handoffs fail, choose a swimlane map built with the frontline staff.` },
      { h: `Does it work, and do they like it`,
        body: [`Effectiveness asks whether the system improves care: fewer errors, faster treatment. Satisfaction asks how it is to use: surveys, interviews, click counts, time spent in the record, help desk calls. Measure both against a baseline. New technology gets judged the same way. Telehealth, telepharmacy, remote monitoring, portals, wearables, and population health tools earn a place by outcomes, not by being new.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"A surgeon counting clicks is a survey with one person in it,"</span> Ochoa said, and handed back the pad. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Don't laugh at it. Find out whether he's the one or the hundred."</span>`],
        terms: [[`User satisfaction`,`How users experience a system, measured with surveys, interviews, usage data, and observation.`],[`Telepharmacy`,`Pharmacists reviewing orders or counseling patients remotely, often for small or rural sites.`]],
        exam: `Effectiveness and satisfaction are measured against a baseline. A trend is worth adopting when it improves outcomes for a real gap.` }
    ],
    video: [`9e9Rtl9_fKc`,`Barcode Medication Administration`,`UKHealthCare`,`4:29`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f4s1`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L6`, title:`Forty-seven`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Walt Hagen was in the surgeons' lounge with the complaint, a pencil, and a printed discharge summary with tally marks down the margin. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, flat, pointed.">"Forty-seven,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I counted again this morning, in case you thought I was exaggerating."</span>`,`He walked her through the new ones. A screen to confirm he'd seen every pending result. Two clicks to acknowledge each one. A medication count to confirm on transfers. She knew all six. She'd sat in the room where they were decided.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I'm told these were safety fixes. I believe it. I'd like to know which of my forty-seven are keeping someone safe, and which are keeping someone covered."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Explain that the six came from the safety review, and ask him to give them time.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Walt nods slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Give it time. They said that in 1998."</span> He doesn't argue. He also stops telling her what's slow.`,
          why:`The reason is true, and he deserved to hear it. But you asked a skeptic to trust you instead of showing him. Measure each click against its purpose, then you'll know which ones deserve his patience.`},
        {t:`Have Kiran take out the six new clicks this week, to win back his trust fast.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Kiran pulls them Wednesday. Walt is pleased for a week. Then a patient goes home with a potassium still pending that nobody looked at, and the review that added the check opens again.`,
          why:`You undid a safety fix to answer a complaint you hadn't measured. Some of those clicks protect patients. Find out which before you touch any of them.`},
        {t:`Sit with him through a real discharge, and tag what every single click is for.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`She follows him through a gallbladder discharge at ten, legal pad on her knee. Each click gets one word: safety, billing, legal, habit, nothing. The pending results check stays, and he agrees it should. Kiran folds its two acknowledgments into one signed list, and an old duplicate diagnosis entry goes. Walt looks at the new count for a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Grudging, quiet, almost approving.">"You kept the ones that matter and told me why. That's new."</span> At the door he turns. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Quiet, pointed, deliberate.">"While you're counting, count the nurses."</span>`,
          why:`User satisfaction is data, and one surgeon's count is a place to start. Watch the real work, measure each step against its purpose, keep what protects patients, and say why. Effectiveness and satisfaction get measured together.`},
        {t:`Send him the annual user survey, so his complaint is counted with everyone's.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Walt puts the link in his pocket with the printed note. The survey asks him to rate his satisfaction from one to five. It doesn't ask which clicks.`,
          why:`Surveys matter at scale. A specific complaint with a specific count deserves a specific look. A score won't tell you what to fix.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s2`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C1`, title:`Six U`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday afternoon Renee pulled up an order on the MedaLink screen and turned it toward Maria without a word.`,`It read: insulin regular 6U SubQ AC. Below it, warfarin 5.0 mg PO QD.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Night shift read the first one as sixty last spring,"</span> Renee said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Pharmacy caught it. The doctor didn't type it like that. He picked it off a list. They're all built like that."</span>`,`Kiran checked from Fort Collins while she waited. Laramie's order sentence library, built in 2011, had 312 sentences with a U, a QD, a QOD, or a trailing zero. About 140 active orders had been written from them.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Kiran rewrite all 312 sentences himself tonight, using the standard terms.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Kiran finishes at 2 AM. Two of his rewrites change the meaning: an every other day dose becomes daily. Pharmacy finds them a week later. And the 140 active orders still say QD.`,
          why:`Right fix, wrong path. Order content belongs to clinicians with pharmacy checking it, and changing the library doesn't change the orders already running.`},
        {t:`Post a reminder at each nurses' station listing which terms to read carefully.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The signs go up Tuesday. By March they're under a flu shot flyer.`,
          why:`A sign asks nurses to catch an error your build keeps creating. Fix the build. Don't make the bedside the last defense against your order sentences.`},
        {t:`Leave MedaLink as it is, and make sure One Chart's order sentences are built clean.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`For eighteen months, Wyoming orders keep saying U and QD. Nobody counts the near misses.`,
          why:`A live safety hazard doesn't wait for a replacement project. Fix it now, and One Chart gets the clean list for free.`},
        {t:`Take it to the council and pharmacy, fix every sentence, and redo the live orders.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`Aisha puts it on the next council agenda as urgent. Pharmacy and nursing review the list in a week: U becomes units, QD becomes daily, trailing zeros go. Kiran builds, and pharmacy checks each sentence. Then pharmacy rewrites the 140 active orders one by one, and Renee tapes the old screenshot inside the med room door with a line through it.`,
          why:`Some abbreviations cause errors often enough to be on The Joint Commission's do-not-use list. The list formally covers handwritten, free-text, and preprinted orders, not pick lists built into an EHR, but the expectation is to strip the terms whenever you build or upgrade, and these sentences put them on every order a nurse reads. Fix the source through clinical governance, then clean up the orders already live.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s3`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C4`, title:`The med pass`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday at nine Maria followed Jess, a nurse five years out of school, on her morning med pass. Six patients, twenty-two doses. Jess scanned the wristband, scanned each medication, and MedaLink checked them against the eMAR. Green, green, green.`,`Room 312's wristband had been through two days of hand sanitizer and a shower. The scanner beeped and failed. Jess tried twice, then clicked a button at the bottom of the eMAR labeled Given, no scan. The dose was charted. She was on to the next one in ten seconds.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Everybody uses it when the band won't read,"</span> Jess said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Scanning's six clicks when it works. That button is two. The night nurses built it, years ago."</span> She didn't sound defensive. She sounded like someone describing the weather.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell Jess that skipping the scan is against policy, and that she has to stop.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Jess says she will. She scans carefully for the rest of the pass, with Maria watching. Maria will never know what she does on Wednesday.`,
          why:`You turned an observation into an audit. People hide workarounds from auditors, and you need them to show you, so you can fix what drives them.`},
        {t:`Check that dose with Jess, then finish the pass and log each skip and why.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She asks Jess to read the five rights on that dose, out loud, against the eMAR. All right. Then she keeps walking and keeps a tally. Five of twenty-two doses skip the scan: three bands that won't read, a liquid from pharmacy with no barcode, a scanner battery that dies in 316. At the end Jess looks wary. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Wary, surprised, quiet.">"You're not writing me up?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I'm fixing the bands,"</span> Maria says. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"And the button."</span>`,
          why:`Patient safety first: confirm the dose in front of you is right, and reprint the band or confirm two identifiers before the next dose. Then watch the real work without punishing the person showing it to you. Count the workarounds and learn why each one happens. That's where the fixes are.`},
        {t:`Have the charge nurse reprint every wristband on the unit before the noon meds.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Twenty-six new bands by noon. By Thursday half of them are smeared again, and the liquid still has no barcode.`,
          why:`Reprinting helps today. It doesn't ask why bands stop reading in two days, and it misses the other reasons doses skip the scan.`},
        {t:`Report the button to the nurse manager, so she can coach the unit tomorrow.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The manager coaches the unit. The button gets used less for a week, then as much as ever. Now nobody mentions it while Maria's around.`,
          why:`Coaching a workaround away without fixing its causes just moves it out of sight. Find out why before anyone gets coached.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s4`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C4`, title:`Walt's tally`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday afternoon Walt found her in the cafeteria and set a printed note on her tray, face down. On the back, in pencil, were tally marks.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, grave, deliberate.">"You counted my clicks. I counted your nurses. Three days on my floor. Fifty-eight doses I watched. Eleven went through that button."</span>`,`He'd brought the monthly quality report from the unit break room, too.`,
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="BCMA scan compliance, December, as reported" labels="Corvane hospitals,Cheyenne,Laramie,Rawlins" values="93,96,97,98">Percent of doses with both the wristband and the medication scanned, from the monthly quality report. The three MedaLink hospitals report the highest rates in the system.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Your oldest system has your best numbers,"</span> Walt said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Low, grave, deliberate.">"In my line of work, when the chart looks better than the patient, you look again."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Thank him, and explain that nursing quality checks the report every month.`, s:0, flag:`dismissedwalt`, d:{trust:-6,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Walt takes the paper back, folds it, and puts it in his coat. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Flat, cold, disappointed.">"Checked every month,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Flat, quiet, done.">"Okay."</span> He doesn't bring her anything else that week.`,
          why:`You defended a report you hadn't looked at against a man who had. The question was never whether someone reviews it. It was what it counts.`},
        {t:`Pass his tally to the Laramie CNO, since BCMA practice belongs to nursing.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The CNO thanks her and puts it on the nursing quality agenda for March. The report keeps saying 97.`,
          why:`Nursing owns the practice. But the report is built in your systems, and whether it counts every dose is a data question you can answer this week.`},
        {t:`Tell him he may be right, and have Kiran pull every dose by how it was charted.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Kiran pulls December at Laramie, every dose and every path it took into the chart. It takes him until midnight, and he doesn't mention that. Walt counted one in five going around the scanner. The data says one in six. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Close enough,"</span> Walt says when she tells him. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I'm a surgeon, not a statistician."</span>`,
          why:`When a clinician's count disagrees with your dashboard, check the data before you defend it. Pull every dose by how it was charted and compare it with how the report counts. A skeptic who counts is a free auditor.`},
        {t:`Have Kiran remove the Given, no scan button tonight at all three hospitals.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`By morning nurses can't chart a dose when a band won't read. Some chart on paper, some wait, and a patient in Cheyenne gets a second dose of a sedative because the first was never charted.`,
          why:`Taking away a workaround before fixing its causes leaves nurses with no safe path. The doses still happen. Now they're just invisible.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C2`, title:`Paper orders`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday at 7:40 Maria sat at the surgery unit desk beside Pat, the unit clerk, who'd held the job for twenty-six years. A stack of paper came up from the OR. Post-op orders, handwritten on a preprinted form, signed. Pat typed them into MedaLink one at a time and picked the surgeon's name as the ordering provider.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Dr. Hagen's,"</span> Pat said, holding one up. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Best handwriting in the building."</span>`,`Cascade's annual report to the board listed all eleven hospitals as CPOE live. Kiran checked. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"It is live,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Surgery just doesn't use it much."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Count clerk-entered orders as CPOE, since each lists the surgeon who wrote it.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`Laramie's CPOE rate stays at a hundred. The surgeons never see an allergy alert, because they never touch the system, and nobody reading the report knows.`,
          why:`The ordering provider's name on the order doesn't make it CPOE. The point of CPOE is that the prescriber sees the checks while they can still change the order.`},
        {t:`Report CPOE as the share of orders prescribers enter, with Laramie's real rate.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Kiran pulls it: last quarter, 38 percent of Laramie surgical orders were entered by the prescriber. Maria sends Ochoa the number with two lines on what CPOE means. He puts it in the next board report as written. Then she asks Walt why he writes on paper. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"The post-op order set is thirty-four clicks. Paper takes forty seconds."</span> She writes that down too.`,
          why:`Know the words exactly. CPOE means the prescriber enters the order directly, so dose checks and allergy alerts reach the person who can act on them. Transcription isn't CPOE. Measure it as a rate, and report what's true.`},
        {t:`Require Laramie surgeons to enter all their own orders, starting next month.`, s:1, d:{trust:-3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The surgeons push back, Walt first. Paper orders keep coming, and some arrive as verbal orders a nurse enters instead, which is worse.`,
          why:`The goal is right. A mandate without fixing a thirty-four click order set pushes the work into verbal orders, which carry more risk. Find out why they don't use it, then fix that.`},
        {t:`Leave the board metric alone, since One Chart will make it moot within a year.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The board keeps reading CPOE live. When One Chart's business case claims a safety gain from CPOE at Laramie, there's no honest baseline to measure it against.`,
          why:`A metric you know is misleading should be corrected now. You'll also need the true baseline to prove what One Chart changes.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C3`, title:`The denominator`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Kiran found it Wednesday night. MedaLink's BCMA report had been built in 2016 by an analyst long gone. It divided scanned doses by doses charted through the scanning screen. The Given, no scan button, built by Laramie's night nurses that same year, charted doses through a different screen. The report had never seen them.`,`Laramie, December: the report said 97 percent. Counting every dose given, it was 81.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Both numbers are real,"</span> Kiran said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"One of them just answers a question nobody asked."</span> The next report was due to the board's quality committee on February 1.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fix the report quietly so February shows the true rate, and explain if asked.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`February shows 81 with no explanation. The Laramie CNO gets a call from the quality committee asking what went wrong on her units in January. Nothing did.`,
          why:`Fixing the math is right. Changing a reported number without saying why makes care look worse overnight and costs you the trust of the people whose number it is.`},
        {t:`Keep the report as is until One Chart, since changing the method breaks the trend.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Every month the board sees 97, and every month you know it's 81.`,
          why:`A trend built on the wrong denominator isn't worth protecting. Once you know a safety metric is overstated, keeping it is a choice to mislead.`},
        {t:`Take both numbers to nursing quality, agree on a definition, and restate openly.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The nursing quality director owns the metric. She, the three CNOs, and Kiran agree on one definition: scanned doses over every dose charted, by any path. The February report shows both rates for December side by side, with a paragraph on why. The committee asks hard questions, and Ochoa answers them beside her. Nobody's number dropped. It had always been 81.`,
          why:`Read the denominator first. The owner of a metric sets its definition, IT builds it, and when a reported number was wrong you restate it openly, old and new side by side, so nobody thinks care got worse overnight.`},
        {t:`Send the true rate to each CNO, and ask each one to explain why it's lower.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Three CNOs spend a week defending their nurses against a number that was never about their nurses. Two of them stop returning Kiran's calls.`,
          why:`The gap is in the report, not the units. Asking people to explain a measurement error puts the blame in the wrong place.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s7`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA4`, title:`Butcher paper`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday morning I-80 was closed between Laramie and Rawlins, blowing snow at Elk Mountain and a jackknifed truck. Rawlins would have to be a video call.`,`So Maria had a free morning in Laramie and a conference room with a long blank wall. Jess and Renee came in on their break, with a pharmacist from downstairs. Kiran joined on a laptop propped against the coffee pot. She wanted to see the whole medication process at Laramie in one place before anyone fixed a piece of it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Start from Corvane's standard medication workflow, and mark where Laramie is different.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The diagram is clean and has no button labeled Given, no scan. The nurses spend an hour explaining what isn't on it, then stop.`,
          why:`A vendor's diagram shows the process as designed somewhere else. You need this process as it runs here, workarounds included.`},
        {t:`Draw the One Chart future state first, so the group has a goal to work toward.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The future state looks good on the wall. Nobody can say which of today's problems it fixes, because today isn't on the wall.`,
          why:`The future state matters, but it comes second. Map the current state first, so the gap between them is something you can see.`},
        {t:`Map today's real process in swimlanes with the nurses, workarounds included.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Five lanes on butcher paper: surgeon, clerk, pharmacy, cabinet, nurse. Paper order, typed in, verified, pulled, scanned, given, charted. Then the diamonds. The band that won't read and the arrow down to Given, no scan. The liquid with no barcode. The pharmacist who isn't there at night, so the cabinet runs on override. By noon the wall shows three places where a dose goes around the scanner. Renee takes a photo. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"First time anybody drew it the way we do it."</span>`,
          why:`Map the current state as it actually runs, with the people who run it, before designing the future state. A swimlane map shows who does each step and where the handoffs and workarounds are. Then draw the future state you want. The gap between the two is your gap analysis.`},
        {t:`Rank every reason for a skipped scan in a chart, from most to least common.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:2,health:1},
          after:`The chart is useful. Bands lead, then barcodes, then batteries. It doesn't show where in the process each one happens, or who could fix it.`,
          why:`Ranking causes is a good tool for deciding what to fix first. It works best after you've mapped the process, so you know where each cause lives.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s8`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E5`, title:`The night pharmacist`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The Rawlins CNO, Gail, came on the video call that afternoon from her office, with a window behind her full of snow. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"We'd have loved to have you. The road had other plans."</span>`,`Rawlins had twenty-two beds and one doctor in the building at night. It hadn't had a night pharmacist since last January, when the last one retired. After five o'clock, nurses pulled first doses from the cabinet on override, and a Cheyenne pharmacist reviewed the night's orders in the morning.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Gail">"I know it's not ideal,"</span> Gail said. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"But our scan rate's the best in the system. Ninety-eight. We're careful."</span> She'd found a telepharmacy company online that could verify orders overnight, and she wanted to know if Maria would sign off.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell Rawlins to keep the morning review, since One Chart will fix the cabinets.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`Gail says okay in a voice that means she's heard that before. Every night for another eighteen months, first doses go in before a pharmacist sees them.`,
          why:`A replacement project doesn't fix a live gap in medication safety. And this one isn't an EHR problem. It's a missing check you can add now.`},
        {t:`Approve the telepharmacy company Rawlins found, and get a contract signed this month.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`Pharmacy and Joan both stop it a week later. The company has never connected to MedaLink, nobody checked its security, and there's no business associate agreement yet.`,
          why:`Telepharmacy is the right idea. An outside vendor still has to go through review, and you may already own a better answer.`},
        {t:`Have the Rawlins night doctor double check her own orders before the nurse pulls.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The doctor is gracious about it. A prescriber checking her own order catches what she already knew and misses what she didn't.`,
          why:`Pharmacist verification is valuable because it's a second, independent check by a different expert. Self review isn't a substitute.`},
        {t:`Work with pharmacy on night verification from Cheyenne, under state pharmacy rules.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:6},
          after:`Cascade's pharmacy director takes it on. Cheyenne already has a pharmacist overnight. With access to Rawlins orders in MedaLink and the state pharmacy board's rules checked, remote verification can start in March. Night first doses will get a pharmacist's eyes before the cabinet opens. Gail is quiet a second. <span class="said" data-who="Gail" data-tone="Quiet, rueful, relieved.">"We asked for this two years ago."</span> Maria believes her.`,
          why:`Telepharmacy is a trend that improves outcomes for small hospitals: a pharmacist checks each order before the dose, even with nobody on site at night. Judge it by outcomes, use what you already own when you can, and check the state rules.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s9`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C1`, title:`Nine and nine`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, Cheyenne. Denise Harmon met her at the elevator. <span class="said" data-who="Denise">"You're in my building. I hear you've been counting things."</span> Denise had led the nurses against Penwright two years ago and ended up its best champion. Now she was a One Chart superuser, and she'd read the draft frequency map Kiran's team sent out for review.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Denise">"You mapped MedaLink BID to Corvane BID. Up here BID means nine and nine. Breakfast and bedtime. Down there BID is nine and five, so the evening dose lands at dinner. And half our q12h drugs were built as BID in 2014 because it was easier. Map those by name and you just moved every med pass on my floor, and you changed when people's heparin is due."</span>`,`Her phone buzzed while Denise was still talking.`,
        `<prop type="text" from="Lucia" time="10:14 AM">mom is BID the same as q12h. my practice quiz says no and i think the quiz is wrong</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Map each frequency by its real times and intent, with pharmacy and nursing.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Pharmacy pulls every active frequency at the three hospitals, and each one is mapped by what it means: how many doses, at what times, and whether the timing matters. Fourteen MedaLink BID orders turn out to be q12h drugs and get mapped that way. The question of standard times goes to the council. Denise reads the final map and signs it. <span class="said" data-who="Denise">"Now that I'll train."</span>`,
          why:`Clinical vocabulary is precise. BID is twice a day at set times. q12h is every twelve hours by the clock, and some drugs need it. Map by meaning with the people who own it, and let governance set the standard times.`},
        {t:`Map them by name, since BID means twice a day in every system that exists.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`The map passes review on paper. At go-live, every evening BID dose at Cheyenne moves four hours earlier, and fourteen heparin orders lose their twelve hour spacing.`,
          why:`The abbreviation is the same, and the meaning isn't. Converting clinical data by label instead of meaning is how a migration moves doses without anyone deciding to.`},
        {t:`Keep MedaLink's nine and nine as a local BID for the three Wyoming hospitals.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Med pass times stay put, which Denise appreciates. The fourteen q12h drugs built as BID are still built as BID, and now One Chart has two kinds of BID.`,
          why:`Protecting the floor's routine is reasonable. But a local variant hides the real problem, which is orders whose meaning was wrong. Map by intent, then let governance settle the times.`},
        {t:`Let Corvane's default times stand, and retrain Wyoming nurses at go-live.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Denise looks at her for a long moment. <span class="said" data-who="Denise" data-tone="Dry, flat, unimpressed.">"You've met my nurses, right?"</span> The q12h drugs built as BID go live wrong anyway.`,
          why:`Retraining people around a mapping you haven't checked puts the burden on the floor and leaves the real errors in place.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s10`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C3`, title:`Heads in beds`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday afternoon, in Denise's office, Kiran came on speakerphone. To size the One Chart training plan, he needed one number per hospital: average daily census. It would set how many superusers each unit needed at the elbow on go-live weekend, and how many scanners to order.`,`He had three reports and three numbers for Rawlins in December. Finance said ten. Nursing's staffing report said fourteen. The bed board said sixteen.`,`Gail called in from Rawlins. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"They're all real. We just count different heads. Finance counts acute inpatients. We count the swing beds too, because we staff them. The bed board counts observation patients, because they're in a bed."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use the highest number, sixteen, so the plan never comes up short at go-live.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Rawlins gets enough superusers. Cheyenne's plan uses finance's number, though, so the two hospitals can't be compared, and nobody notices until the scanners arrive.`,
          why:`Erring toward enough support is sensible. Picking numbers built on different definitions makes every comparison wrong. Define it once.`},
        {t:`Average the three reports for each hospital, and use that in the training plan.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Rawlins becomes 13.3, a census that matches nothing anyone counts.`,
          why:`Averaging three definitions gives you a fourth one nobody uses. Decide what you're counting, then count it.`},
        {t:`Agree with the data owners which patients count, write it down, and use it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:3},
          after:`Finance, nursing, and HIM agree in one call: inpatient days count acute and swing bed patients, observation is reported separately. It goes in writing. Rawlins is fourteen, and its plan adds support for the observation patients the nurses chart on too. Every hospital uses the same rule.`,
          why:`Census is inpatient days divided by days in the period, and "inpatient day" needs a definition. Departments count differently for good reasons. Agree with the data's owners, write it down, and use it everywhere so numbers compare.`},
        {t:`Use finance's number, since it's the one the board and the auditors already see.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Rawlins is planned for ten. On go-live weekend there are sixteen patients in beds and two superusers short.`,
          why:`The right definition depends on the question. Staffing a go-live means counting every patient nurses chart on. Pick the definition that fits the use, and write it down.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s11`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C3`, title:`First dose`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, January 26, Aisha called while Maria was eating lunch at her desk. Her sepsis alert was going to the council next week, and she wanted a baseline: order to first dose of antibiotics, all eleven hospitals.`,`Kiran had the data and a problem. In Corvane, the time given came from the scan. At the MedaLink hospitals, Given, no scan let the nurse type in the time. One Laramie record showed an antibiotic given two minutes before it was ordered.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Just give me the average,"</span> Aisha said. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"I need one number for the council."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Give her the mean for all eleven hospitals, with every record included as charted.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`The council sees a respectable average. It hides typed-in times that run early and a few patients who waited four hours.`,
          why:`An average of mixed quality data hides both the bad data and the slowest patients. Turnaround needs clear timestamps and a look at the tail.`},
        {t:`Drop every record with a typed-in time, and report only doses with a scan time.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The numbers are cleaner. They also leave out a sixth of Laramie's doses and a third of Rawlins', mostly at night, which is when the waits are longest.`,
          why:`Excluding weak data is better than mixing it in silently. But dropping it skews the sample toward the easy cases. Show it separately instead.`},
        {t:`Define start and stop, report the median and slow tail, and flag typed times.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The clock starts when the order is signed and stops when the first dose is charted. Median, plus the 90th percentile, because sepsis kills the patients who wait longest. Typed-in times show up in their own column. Laramie's median is 74 minutes by scan and 51 by typed time, and nobody believes the 51. Aisha studies it. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"So the button's in my data too."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It's in everybody's,"</span> Maria says.`,
          why:`Turnaround time needs a defined start and stop, from timestamps that mean the same thing everywhere. Report the median and a slow percentile, and label weaker data instead of mixing it in or throwing it out.`},
        {t:`Tell Aisha the MedaLink data can't be trusted, and baseline only Corvane sites.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The baseline covers eight hospitals. The three that need sepsis help most are left out of it.`,
          why:`Data with known limits can still inform a decision if you label the limits. Leaving out the hospitals with the biggest gaps hides the problem you were asked to measure.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s12`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E6`, title:`Which record is true`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, January 28. The restated report would go to the committee on Monday. Kiran wanted to go further: the true rate at all three MedaLink hospitals, month by month, for the past year.`,`He had an idea to make it faster. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"The dispensing cabinets log every removal with a time and a patient. Cleaner than MedaLink. I could just count those as the doses given."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use the cabinet removals for speed, and note that the counts are approximate.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It's fast. It counts doses that were pulled and wasted, pulled and returned, or pulled and never given, and misses floor stock that never came from a cabinet.`,
          why:`Cabinet data is real and useful. It measures removal, not administration. Approximate is fine when you say so, but here the error lands right where you're looking.`},
        {t:`Ask the cabinet vendor to build a scan compliance report from its own logs.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The vendor quotes eight weeks. And the cabinet still has no idea whether anyone scanned a wristband.`,
          why:`The cabinet can't see the bedside. Ask each application for what it actually records.`},
        {t:`Use the eMAR as the record of what was given, and the cabinet to cross-check.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Kiran builds it in the warehouse: every eMAR dose by how it was charted, joined to cabinet removals to find doses pulled and never charted, and charted and never pulled. It takes him four nights. He sends the file at 11:52 PM on Monday, February 1, with no message at all.`,
          why:`Know what each application is the source of truth for. The eMAR in the clinical record documents administration. The pharmacy cabinet documents removal. The warehouse joins them for analysis.`},
        {t:`Have nurse managers count scans by hand from a sample of charts at each site.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Three managers lose a week each to chart review. The sample is small and the method differs by hospital.`,
          why:`Manual audits have their place, like observation. When the full data already sits in your systems, use it.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f4s6`, title:`Scan compliance, counted two ways`, setup:[`Laramie, December. The old report divided scanned doses by doses charted through the scanning screen. The Given, no scan button charted doses through a different screen, so the report never counted them.`,`Doses charted through the scanning screen: 15,000. Of those, both wristband and medication scanned: 14,550. Doses charted with Given, no scan: 3,000.`,`BCMA scan compliance = scanned doses / all doses given. Round percents to two decimals.`],
      fields:[{label:`Rate the old report showed, percent`, answer:97, tol:0.01},{label:`True rate, counting every dose, percent`, answer:80.83, tol:0.01},{label:`Gap between them, percentage points`, answer:16.17, tol:0.02},{label:`Additional scanned doses a month to reach 95 percent`, answer:2550, tol:0}],
      solution:[`Old report: 14,550 / 15,000 = 0.97, or 97.00 percent.`,`All doses given: 15,000 + 3,000 = 18,000. True rate: 14,550 / 18,000 = 0.80833, or 80.83 percent.`,`Gap: 97.00 minus 80.83 = 16.17 percentage points.`,`To reach 95 percent of 18,000 doses: 0.95 x 18,000 = 17,100 scanned doses. 17,100 minus 14,550 = 2,550 more a month.`,`Same scans, same nurses, two numbers. The only difference is the denominator. That's why the definition belongs to the metric's owner and goes in writing.`] },
    { after:`f4s10`, title:`Average daily census`, setup:[`December has 31 days. Cheyenne recorded 4,650 inpatient days. Rawlins, with 22 staffed beds, recorded 434 inpatient days, counting acute and swing bed patients. Of those, 124 were swing bed days. Laramie recorded 1,550 inpatient days and 310 discharges.`,`Average daily census = total inpatient days / days in the period. Occupancy = census / staffed beds. Average length of stay, using the common shortcut = inpatient days / discharges. Round to two decimals.`],
      fields:[{label:`Cheyenne average daily census`, answer:150, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins average daily census, acute and swing`, answer:14, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins occupancy, percent`, answer:63.64, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins average daily census, acute only`, answer:10, tol:0.01},{label:`Laramie average length of stay, days`, answer:5, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`Cheyenne: 4,650 / 31 = 150 patients a day.`,`Rawlins, acute and swing: 434 / 31 = 14.`,`Rawlins occupancy: 14 / 22 = 0.63636, or 63.64 percent.`,`Rawlins, acute only: 434 minus 124 = 310 inpatient days. 310 / 31 = 10. That's finance's number. Same hospital, same month, different definition.`,`Laramie length of stay: 1,550 / 310 = 5.00 days.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f4s2`, title:`Read the order`, intro:`Analysts build what clinicians write, so the words have to be exact. Pick what each order means.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`metoprolol 25 mg PO BID`, choices:[`By mouth, twice a day`,`Into a vein, every 12 hours`,`Under the tongue, daily`,`By mouth, every 2 hours`], answer:0, why:`PO is by mouth. BID is twice a day, given at the hospital's standard times.`},
      {prompt:`hydromorphone 0.5 mg IV q3h PRN severe pain`, choices:[`Every three hours, around the clock`,`Three times a day, as needed`,`As needed, no more often than every three hours`,`Once now, then every three hours`], answer:2, why:`PRN means as needed. q3h sets the minimum interval. A complete PRN order also states the reason, here severe pain.`},
      {prompt:`enoxaparin 40 mg SubQ daily`, choices:[`Into a muscle`,`Under the skin`,`Into a vein`,`On the skin`], answer:1, why:`SubQ is subcutaneous, an injection under the skin. IM is into a muscle and IV into a vein.`},
      {prompt:`Which of these belongs on the do-not-use list?`, choices:[`mg`,`PRN`,`daily`,`QD`], answer:3, why:`QD is on The Joint Commission's do-not-use list because it's confused with QOD. Write daily.`},
      {prompt:`insulin lispro per scale SubQ AC and HS`, choices:[`After meals and at bedtime`,`Twice a day with meals`,`Before meals and at bedtime`,`As needed for high sugar`], answer:2, why:`AC is before meals and HS is at bedtime. PC would mean after meals.`},
      {prompt:`furosemide 40 mg IV STAT`, choices:[`Now, right away`,`Every morning`,`Before meals`,`At bedtime`], answer:0, why:`STAT means give it immediately.`},
      {prompt:`A wound care order for a pressure injury on the heel concerns which body system?`, choices:[`Musculoskeletal`,`Integumentary`,`Cardiovascular`,`Endocrine`], answer:1, why:`The integumentary system is the skin. Pressure injuries are skin and tissue damage.`},
      {prompt:`A heparin drip adjusted by clotting time lab results concerns which body system?`, choices:[`Respiratory`,`Renal`,`Neurologic`,`Hematologic`], answer:3, why:`Anticoagulants act on the blood's clotting, which is the hematologic system.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Which frequency means a dose is given only when needed, no more often than a stated interval?`, opts:[`STAT`,`PRN`,`BID`,`AC`], a:1, why:`PRN means as needed. A complete PRN order states the reason and the minimum time between doses. STAT is now, BID is twice a day, AC is before meals.`, task:`C1`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A legacy EHR's order sentence library uses "U" for units and "QD" for daily, and active orders were written from it. What is the BEST action?`, opts:[`Post reminders about these terms on every unit`,`Wait and build clean sentences in the new EHR`,`Rebuild via governance and fix the live orders`,`Have one analyst correct the library tonight`], a:2, why:`Both are on The Joint Commission's do-not-use list. The list doesn't formally cover preprogrammed EHR pick lists, but organizations are expected to remove these terms when they build or upgrade, and the orders display them to everyone who reads them. Fix the source through clinical governance with pharmacy checking, then correct the orders already active.`, task:`C1`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`During a conversion, an analyst plans to map every legacy BID frequency to the new system's BID. What is the GREATEST risk?`, opts:[`Admin times and intent may change unreviewed`,`The new system may not support BID orders`,`Nurses will need to relearn the abbreviation`,`Pharmacy will have to reprint the barcodes`], a:0, why:`Frequencies must be mapped by meaning. Standard times can differ by site, and some orders built as BID may need q12h spacing.`, task:`C1`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A hospital recorded 1,240 inpatient days in a 31-day month. What was its average daily census?`, opts:[`36`,`40`,`42`,`44`], a:1, why:`Average daily census = inpatient days / days in the period. 1,240 / 31 = 40.`, task:`C3`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A BCMA report shows 97 percent scan compliance, but doses charted through an alternate screen aren't in its denominator. What should the leader do FIRST?`, opts:[`Recognize the units for exceeding the target`,`Remove the alternate charting path at once`,`Ask the vendor to certify the report logic`,`Recalculate counting every dose, by any path`], a:3, why:`Check the denominator. Compliance is scanned doses over all doses given. Recalculate, then have the metric's owner set the definition and restate openly.`, task:`C3`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Leaders want a baseline for order to first antibiotic dose. Some administration times were typed in by nurses. Which approach is BEST?`, opts:[`Report the mean of every record, exactly as it was charted`,`Report the median and 90th percentile, flagging typed times`,`Drop every record with a typed time, then report the rest`,`Report only the sites whose times come from the scanner`], a:1, why:`Turnaround needs a defined start and stop. The median and a slow percentile show typical and worst waits. Label weaker data rather than mixing it in or dropping it.`, task:`C3`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Nurses often use an override to chart doses when wristbands won't scan. What is the BEST response?`, opts:[`Coach the staff who use the override`,`Remove the override from the eMAR now`,`Find and fix the reasons scans fail`,`Add a warning pop-up to the override`], a:2, why:`A workaround signals a design or equipment problem. Find the causes, like bands that smear or meds with no barcode, and fix them. Removing the path or punishing staff drives it underground.`, task:`C4`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Surgeons handwrite post-op orders that a unit clerk enters under the surgeon's name. How should these orders be counted?`, opts:[`As CPOE, since the surgeon is listed as orderer`,`As transcribed orders, which don't count as CPOE`,`As verbal orders that still need a read-back`,`As standing protocol orders, signed later on`], a:1, why:`CPOE means the prescriber enters the order directly and sees the decision support. CMS's CPOE measures also count entry by licensed staff and credentialed medical assistants under the prescriber's direction, but a unit clerk transcribing paper still isn't CPOE, whatever name is on the order.`, task:`C2`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which source BEST documents that a dose was actually given to a patient?`, opts:[`The dispensing cabinet removal log`,`The pharmacy's verification queue`,`The eMAR in the patient's record`,`The data warehouse's nightly copy`], a:2, why:`The eMAR is the record of administration. The cabinet records removal, pharmacy records verification, and the warehouse copies data for analysis.`, task:`E6`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A 22-bed rural hospital has no pharmacist on site overnight, so first doses are given before any pharmacist review. Which approach BEST closes the gap?`, opts:[`Remote order verification by pharmacists`,`Nurse double-check before cabinet override`,`Prescriber self-review of each night order`,`Morning retrospective review by Cheyenne`], a:0, why:`Telepharmacy lets pharmacists at another site verify orders before the dose. It's a technology trend that directly improves medication safety for small hospitals.`, task:`E5`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Patients at two critical access hospitals are often transferred to a referral hospital on a different EHR. Which practice BEST protects medication safety across the transfer?`, opts:[`Faxing the face sheet ahead of the transfer`,`Calling report to the receiving charge nurse`,`Sending the full chart on a disk with the crew`,`Reconciling a complete med list on arrival`], a:3, why:`Medication reconciliation at every transition, from a complete list, is the core continuity of care practice. The other steps help, but none of them check the medications.`, task:`E2`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A team wants to see who performs each step of medication administration and where work passes between departments. Which tool fits BEST?`, opts:[`Pareto chart`,`Run chart`,`Swimlane map`,`Fishbone diagram`], a:2, why:`A swimlane map gives each role its own lane, so steps and handoffs are visible. Pareto ranks causes, a run chart shows a trend, and a fishbone sorts possible causes.`, task:`SA4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Three months after go-live, nurses rate the new eMAR 4.5 out of 5, but late medication doses haven't moved from the baseline. What does this show?`, opts:[`It's liked, but not yet effective`,`The survey must have been flawed`,`Late doses don't measure an eMAR`,`High ratings prove it's effective`], a:0, why:`User satisfaction and effectiveness are separate measures, and each is checked against a baseline. Nurses like the tool, but it hasn't changed the outcome it was bought for, so look at the workflow behind late doses. A high rating doesn't prove the tool works, and a flat outcome doesn't make the survey wrong.`, task:`L6`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A surgeon says safety changes added six clicks to every discharge. What should the IT leader do FIRST?`, opts:[`Remove the new steps to restore trust`,`Send the annual user satisfaction survey`,`Explain that safety changes are final`,`Observe a discharge and tag each click`], a:3, why:`Measure before you change anything. Observing the real workflow and tagging each step's purpose shows which clicks protect patients and which can go.`, task:`L6`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`An analyst finds that a quality metric reported to the board was overstated for years because of a report error. What is the BEST way to correct it?`, opts:[`Fix the math quietly from next month on`,`Keep the old method to preserve the trend`,`Restate it openly with old and new shown`,`Ask each unit to explain its lower rate`], a:2, why:`Honest reporting means correcting a known error openly, showing old and new side by side with the reason, so leaders don't mistake a measurement fix for a change in care.`, task:`L10`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`In closed loop medication management, which device releases only the medications a pharmacist has verified for that patient?`, opts:[`A profiled dispensing cabinet`,`A smart infusion pump at the bed`,`A handheld barcode scanner`,`An unlocked floor stock cart`], a:0, why:`A profiled automated dispensing cabinet checks the pharmacist's verified orders and releases only those. The pump delivers the dose, the scanner checks patient and drug at the bedside, and floor stock skips the pharmacist entirely, which is why overrides and floor stock are where the loop breaks.`, task:`C4`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A small rural hospital's emergency department sees a few possible stroke patients a month and has no neurologist on staff. Which technology BEST closes that gap?`, opts:[`A stroke alert on the patient portal`,`Home monitoring after each discharge`,`A stroke order set for ED physicians`,`Live video consults with a neurologist`], a:3, why:`Telehealth brings a specialist's exam to a place without one, which is the real gap here. An order set helps the ED physician but doesn't add the expertise, home monitoring comes after the decision that matters, and a portal can't examine anyone. Judge it like any trend, by time to treatment and outcomes.`, task:`E5`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Patients discharged from a referral hospital back to two rural communities are readmitted more often than local patients. Chart review shows their discharge summaries reach the rural doctors days late. What should the leader address FIRST?`, opts:[`Extra follow-up training for rural physicians`,`Same-day summaries to the referring doctors`,`Longer stays before patients travel home`,`A readmission review with the main payers`], a:1, why:`The gap sits between organizations. The review found the summary arriving late, so the fix is a reliable path back to the referring provider at discharge, like a C-CDA summary sent through the HIE. Training, longer stays, and payer reviews don't fix a handoff that never arrives on time.`, task:`E2`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Maria drove home from Cheyenne on Friday night with the heater on high and five days of notes on the seat beside her. Forty-seven clicks. Three hundred and twelve order sentences. Five of twenty-two doses. A wall of butcher paper. Fourteen heparin orders. Most of it was the same lesson from different directions. The system said one thing and the floor did another, and nobody had looked closely enough to see the space between.`,
    (s.score>=30
      ?`Somewhere past Wellington she worked out why the week had gone well. She'd let other people show her things. A nurse showed her a button. A surgeon showed her a tally. A nurse manager in Cheyenne showed her a map that was wrong. She'd spent fourteen years being the one who knew. This week she'd been the one who asked, and she'd learned more.`
      :`Somewhere past Wellington she started the list of things she'd want back. She'd gone to the floor to listen and caught herself defending a few things she should have questioned. Ochoa would point out which ones on Tuesday. He usually didn't have to.`),
    ((s.flags.dismissedwalt||G.dismissedwalt)
      ?`She heard from Renee the next week that Walt had started a tally of his own on the whiteboard in the surgeons' lounge. Doses watched, doses scanned. Above it, in his good handwriting, was one word: Checked. Nobody at Laramie needed it explained.`
      :`Walt emailed the next week, which Renee said he never did. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, grudging, sincere.">"Saw the restated report. 81. Thank you for looking again. My complaint stands. I'll grant that you looked."</span>`),
    `Lucia was on the couch when she got in, the practice quiz still open on her laptop. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"You never answered me. BID and q12h."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Your quiz is right,"</span> Maria said, and sat down next to her. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"BID is twice a day, when the hospital says. q12h is every twelve hours, no matter what. Some drugs don't care. Some do. Heparin cares."</span>`,
    `Lucia clicked to the next question. Maria watched her for a while, the pencil behind her ear, the way she mouthed the words. Five days of asking people about their work, and she'd never asked her own daughter about hers.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, curious, gentle.">"Why nursing? Really."</span>`,
    `Lucia thought about it. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Plain, thoughtful, sure.">"You come home tired,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Plain, thoughtful, sure.">"You never come home sorry you went."</span>`,
    `Maria didn't have anything to say to that. She leaned over and pointed at the next question, and Lucia knew that one too.`,
    `Kiran's file arrived at 11:52 PM on Monday, February 1. She opened it at 5:50 the next morning, before her one-on-one with Ochoa, with the coffee still brewing.`,
    `The Laramie and Cheyenne lines looked the way she expected. Laramie sat around 81 all year. Cheyenne ran in the low 90s, and Denise's floor was higher.`,
    `Rawlins didn't.`,
    `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="Rawlins BCMA scan compliance, counting every dose" labels="Nov 2025,Dec 2025,Jan 2026,Apr 2026,Jul 2026,Oct 2026,Dec 2026" values="91,90,64,62,63,61,62">The true rate, from the eMAR, by month. The report the board saw said 98 percent every month.</prop>`,
    `Ninety in December 2025. Sixty-four in January. It had never come back. For a whole year, more than one dose in three at Rawlins had gone around the scanner, and every month the report had said ninety-eight, and Gail had been proud of it, and so had everyone who read it.`,
    `Maria sat very still. Then she remembered Gail on the video call, a window full of snow behind her, saying it almost in passing. <span class="said" data-who="Gail" data-tone="Offhand, almost in passing.">"We haven't had a night pharmacist since last January."</span>`,
    `She picked up her phone and called Kiran, who answered on the first ring at six in the morning, which told her he hadn't slept either.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, certain, grim.">"Rawlins,"</span> she said.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, flat, grim.">"I know,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Low, flat, stunned.">"A year."</span>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch04.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Alert`
});
